# A parameter's default should reach every generated surface

Found while binding `add_to_store`. The codegen carried a parameter's
NAME and TYPE and nothing else, so a Cython default would have existed
on the sync binding and on none of the four surfaces above it. That is
worse than no default at all: the local call and the remote call would
disagree about what a two-argument call means.

So `Store.add_to_store` shipped with `method` and `hash_algo`
mandatory, and the docstring said why. This closes that.

## Where a default belongs

On the SIGNATURE, and nowhere else.

Every generated surface writes the same default, so a caller who omits
the argument gets the same value in-process and over RPC. By the time
a call reaches the wire, every argument is present - the client filled
it in before the request was built.

That is the whole design, and it is what avoids the alternative. The
alternative was to send absence and resolve it on the server, which
needs field presence: proto3 has none for scalars, so an omitted
string arrives as `""` and is indistinguishable from an empty one
someone meant. Synthesising `proto3_optional` fields would have bought
a distinction nothing needs.

Two consequences worth stating:

- the wire is untouched. No new field, no presence, no schema change.
- the call spec embedded in the RPC client DROPS the default, next to
  the docstring. Carrying it would suggest the runtime fills one in,
  and the runtime never sees one.

## What a default may be

The surfaces write the default as SOURCE, so it must be source they
can write. `model.default_source` decides:

- an enum member becomes the member: `ContentAddressMethod.NAR`. Not
  `repr`, which gives `'nar'` - a StrEnum member IS a string, so that
  calls correctly and a typechecker rejects it.
- a literal becomes its `repr`, checked by reading it back. That is
  not ceremony: `repr(float("inf"))` is `inf`, a NameError in the
  module it would be written into.
- `None` is refused, with a message. It is the one that looks like it
  should work: the surface has no optional spelling, so a None default
  would typecheck here and fail at the first call that took it.
- anything else stops the build, naming the parameter.

An emitted default also feeds import collection. A default names types
just as an annotation does, and the annotation only happens to name
the same one here.

## What holds it

- `test_conformance` compares defaults across protocol, in-process
  wrapper and RPC client, beside names and annotations. A default is
  part of what a call MEANS, so three surfaces agreeing on types and
  differing here answer the same short call differently depending on
  where the object lives. Perturbed: dropping the protocol's defaults
  fails the build naming `Store.add_to_store`.
- `_hierarchy` compares them between a base and its subclasses, for
  the same reason one level down.
- `test_a_default_is_written_or_refused` holds the four rules above.
- two behaviour tests, one local and one remote, assert the short call
  lands on the same store path as the spelled-out one - and that a
  DIFFERENT method gives a different path, so "the default matches
  NAR" cannot pass by the argument being ignored. Perturbed: flipping
  the binding's default to FLAT fails both.

## Done

`Store.add_to_store` defaults to `ContentAddressMethod.NAR` and
`HashAlgorithm.SHA256`. Neither is a judgement made here: both are read
off `addToStoreFromDump`, which declares `hashMethod = NixArchive` and
`hashAlgo = SHA256`. A caller who omits them gets what Nix itself
would have done, which is also what `nix-store --add` does.

## Not done

CONSTRUCTOR defaults still work the other way. `constructor_signature`
derives an `optional` flag from C++ overload arity, and an optional
parameter defaults to None and is skipped on the wire. That mechanism
predates this one and is a different question - a pxd can declare
`f(int x = 3)`, and reading it would make the two agree. Worth doing
when a constructor first wants a real default.

The refusal of a None default is the honest limit, not a design
choice. When the surface learns an optional type - a `dict[str, V]`
value already round-trips absence through `HasField` - a None default
becomes writable and this refusal should go.

It went, for CONTAINERS, in 041. A repeated field has no presence
problem: an absent one and an empty one are the same field, so None
crosses as nothing and reads back as empty. The refusal stands for
every other type, and for the same reason as before.
